1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,206
Today is October 10, 2026.

3
00:00:07,206 --> 00:00:14,204
Overnight, Wall Street mounted a relief rally as crude oil pulled back and software led a technology rebound.

4
00:00:14,204 --> 00:00:21,067
The S and P 500 reclaimed 7,800.3, up over one percent on the week.

5
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:30,773
The Nasdaq added 172 points to 27,366, yet continues to lag its peers below short-term moving averages.

6
00:00:30,773 --> 00:00:35,736
The Dow gained nearly one percent, snapping a five-week losing streak.

7
00:00:35,736 --> 00:00:39,596
Under the surface, liquidity constraints remain tight.

8
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:47,378
Benchmark 30-year Treasury yields broke above 5.6 percent, reaching levels unseen since the turn of the century.

9
00:00:47,378 --> 00:00:54,535
Short-term yields holding near 4.5 percent have driven global money market assets past eight trillion dollars.

10
00:00:54,535 --> 00:00:59,670
Cash now generates over 300 billion dollars in risk-free annual income.

11
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:03,064
That raises the hurdle rate for risk assets.

12
00:01:03,064 --> 00:01:08,395
Macro bulls cite steady two percent GDP growth and near full employment.

13
00:01:08,395 --> 00:01:18,469
Bears highlight deteriorating consumer confidence, where one-year inflation expectations hit 4.7 percent and durable goods demand sits at historic lows.

14
00:01:18,469 --> 00:01:22,353
The market is displaying an extreme structural divergence.

15
00:01:22,353 --> 00:01:30,956
While the S and P index moved less than one percent over the past month, underlying single stocks saw average swings of nearly nine percent.

16
00:01:30,956 --> 00:01:36,434
That level of dispersion ranks in the top five percentile over the past three decades.

17
00:01:36,434 --> 00:01:44,743
Capital is draining out of legacy consumer, energy, and telecom sectors to fund a single massive theme: artificial intelligence.

18
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:51,826
Panic hit semiconductor names earlier when reports pegged OpenAI annualized revenue at 50 billion dollars.

19
00:01:51,826 --> 00:02:00,184
Sentiment reversed once clarified that 50 billion represents net platform take, with 20 billion paid out to partner hyperscalers.

20
00:02:00,184 --> 00:02:04,988
The total ecosystem haul remains intact at 70 billion dollars.

21
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:16,238
Yet institutional players are hedging heavily into late-month catalysts, buying puts across mega-cap tech ahead of earnings, the October Federal Open Market Committee meeting, and midterm elections.

22
00:02:16,238 --> 00:02:19,706
Next week brings the start of third-quarter earnings.

23
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:25,687
Major money-center banks report Tuesday, followed by consumer price index data Wednesday.

24
00:02:25,687 --> 00:02:31,201
In chip manufacturing, ASML and Taiwan Semiconductor report mid-week.

25
00:02:31,201 --> 00:02:35,846
ASML expects revenue up 53 percent year over year.

26
00:02:35,846 --> 00:02:43,775
Taiwan Semiconductor delivered record monthly sales, operating as the undisputed packaging bottleneck for global accelerators.

27
00:02:43,775 --> 00:02:50,589
Looking at key single stocks, Nvidia and the broader semiconductor complex face technical crossroads.

28
00:02:50,589 --> 00:02:57,635
Buyers stepped in to defend moving averages, but crowded positioning means positive surprises are heavily priced in.

29
00:02:57,635 --> 00:03:06,287
Watch the 7,800 S and P floor and semiconductor sector volume for signs of genuine continuation versus tactical relief.

30
00:03:06,287 --> 00:03:16,434
GlobalFoundries traded counter to the semiconductor index, popping on a two-billion-dollar, five-year interposer deal with Taiwan Semiconductor for CoWoS packaging.

31
00:03:16,434 --> 00:03:28,309
However, the stock sits trapped in a 40 to 50 dollar channel, burdened by an 80 percent controlling stake held by Abu Dhabi sovereign wealth, which previously triggered massive insider distribution.

32
00:03:28,309 --> 00:03:32,402
Treat rallies into upper resistance as distribution zones.

33
00:03:32,402 --> 00:03:39,228
Apple slipped over one percent on reports that iPhone 18 Pro build orders were trimmed by up to 20 percent.

34
00:03:39,228 --> 00:03:44,339
Rising DRAM and NAND component costs are pressuring consumer price points.

35
00:03:44,339 --> 00:03:51,128
While intraday dip buyers cushioned the blow, resistance looms overhead until demand metrics stabilize.

36
00:03:51,128 --> 00:03:55,197
Meanwhile, space-based telecom disruption erupted.

37
00:03:55,197 --> 00:04:02,317
SpaceX announced an eight-billion-dollar deal to acquire nationwide 800-megahertz low-band wireless spectrum.

38
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:09,118
The Federal Communications Commission granted authorization for 15,000 direct-to-cell Starlink satellites.

39
00:04:09,118 --> 00:04:11,618
Traditional carriers cratered.

40
00:04:11,618 --> 00:04:19,988
AT and T, Verizon, and T-Mobile shed between 8 and 13 percent as their dividend-heavy moats collapsed under the threat of bypass.

41
00:04:19,988 --> 00:04:26,949
Conversely, cell tower infrastructure plays American Tower and Crown Castle surged up to 15 percent.

42
00:04:26,949 --> 00:04:35,000
Investors bet space constellations must lease ground-station fiber and macro towers rather than build parallel terrestrial networks.

43
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:46,454
Palantir touched fresh all-time highs near 210 dollars following a Wall Street upgrade to 230, driven by rapid enterprise adoption of defense-grade artificial intelligence software.

44
00:04:46,404 --> 00:04:51,343
Valuation risk is acute, demanding strict trailing stop-losses.

45
00:04:51,343 --> 00:04:55,350
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

46
00:04:55,350 --> 00:04:59,517
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
